<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duns Scotus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1265–<a href="/years/1308">1308</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as John Duns Scotus, Doctor Subtilis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Duns Scotus was a Scottish Catholic priest, Franciscan friar, philosopher and theologian, among the most important philosopher-theologians of the late medieval West. Probably from Duns in Berwickshire, he was ordained at Northampton in 1291, studied and taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> and lectured on Peter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a>'s Sentences at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-paris">University of Paris</a>, from which he was expelled for a time for siding with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a> against Philip IV. He is known for the univocity of being, the formal distinction and haecceity, and he argued for the Immaculate Conception of Mary. Called the subtle Doctor, he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> in 1308; his tradition is called Scotism, and he was beatified in 1993.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/duns-scotus.jpg" alt="Duns Scotus" width="330" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Justus van Gent / Pedro Berruguete · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scoto_(Duns_Scoto).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
